# Docker build-context exclusions for the benchmark stack (which uses the lattice repo
# root as build context for the simulator's silo and api images). Exclude bin/obj/test
# artefacts so docker build doesn't ship gigabytes of compile output into the daemon.
#
# PATTERN STYLE IS LOAD-BEARING - read this before editing.
#
# These rules are consumed by two different engines with different dialects, and
# only the intersection of the two is safe:
#
#   * The docker CLI implements the full dockerignore spec, where a trailing
#     slash (`obj/`) and a bare name (`obj`) both match a directory and prune it.
#   * `az acr build` reimplements dockerignore with its own translator, and it
#     converts a rule verbatim into an anchored regex. A trailing slash becomes a
#     trailing `/$`, i.e. `obj/` compiles to `^obj/$` - and because the packer
#     tests paths WITHOUT a trailing slash, that pattern can never match anything.
#     Every trailing-slash rule is silently inert there.
#
# A whole file written in the trailing-slash style therefore looks correct,
# passes review, works locally under `docker build`, and excludes NOTHING during
# a remote ACR build. That is not hypothetical: it let a repo-root-context
# `az acr build` begin packing an 8+ GB upload from a working clone before it was
# caught.
#
# The second half of the quirk: `az acr build` translates a leading `**/` to a
# literal `.*/`, which REQUIRES at least one parent segment. So `**/obj` compiles
# to `^.*/obj$` and does not match a top-level `obj`, where docker's `**/obj`
# would. A directory that can appear at any depth consequently needs all four
# forms below, and dropping any one of them reintroduces a silent gap.
#
# So: no trailing slashes, and for an any-depth directory write
#   name
#   name/**
#   **/name
#   **/name/**
# Verify a change by packing with the same code path az uses, not by reading it:
#   az acr build ... (watch the archive size) - or call
#   azure.cli.command_modules.acr._archive_utils._pack_source_code directly.

# Build output, at any depth.
bin
bin/**
**/bin
**/bin/**
obj
obj/**
**/obj
**/obj/**

# Editor and tool state, at any depth.
.vs
.vs/**
**/.vs
**/.vs/**
.vscode
.vscode/**
**/.vscode
**/.vscode/**
.idea
.idea/**
**/.idea
**/.idea/**
.azurite
.azurite/**
**/.azurite
**/.azurite/**
**/*.user

# Repository metadata - never needed by a build.
.git
.git/**
.github
.github/**

# Test and coverage output, at any depth.
TestResults
TestResults/**
**/TestResults
**/TestResults/**
coverage
coverage/**
**/coverage*
**/coverage*/**
**/results-*.json
benchmark/grafana/dashboards
benchmark/grafana/dashboards/**

# Source-only build contexts: no Dockerfile in this repo needs these trees, and
# packing them is what makes `az acr build` / `docker build` uploads slow.
.scratch
.scratch/**
.tmp
.tmp/**
logs
logs/**
artifacts
artifacts/**
BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts
BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts/**
docs
docs/**
test
test/**
*.log
**/*.log

# Local-only artefact trees. These are gitignored, so they are invisible to a
# `git status` sanity check but are very much visible to the build daemon: on a
# working clone `.deploy/` alone reached ~19 GB of volume and image tarballs,
# which a repo-root-context build would upload in full before running a single
# instruction. `.run/` holds per-run benchmark state written while a rig is up.
.deploy
.deploy/**
**/.deploy
**/.deploy/**
node_modules
node_modules/**
**/node_modules
**/node_modules/**
.run
.run/**
**/.run
**/.run/**
